From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: ps5cemu <ps5cemu@localhost>
Date: Thu, 8 Oct 2026 15:03:31 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] PS5: vertex positions invariant, as on Apple

Pokemon X and Y and Omega Ruby and Alpha Sapphire show moving black
stipples on their outlined models on the console (PS5CEMU-HAR #23), at
any resolution: where two passes compute the same position slightly
differently, parts of a face fail the depth test. Azahar already declares
gl_Position invariant on Apple GPUs for this, whose compilers optimize
more aggressively than desktop drivers; the console's does too, and the
qualifier removed the stipples there in both games. So the PS5 build
declares it as the Apple one does, always. The generated source changes
with it, so the disk shader cache builds those shaders again rather than
reusing the old kind.
---
 src/video_core/shader/generator/glsl_shader_gen.cpp | 4 +++-
 1 file changed, 3 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/src/video_core/shader/generator/glsl_shader_gen.cpp b/src/video_core/shader/generator/glsl_shader_gen.cpp
index 6de5f96b9..8c605530a 100644
--- a/src/video_core/shader/generator/glsl_shader_gen.cpp
+++ b/src/video_core/shader/generator/glsl_shader_gen.cpp
@@ -75,7 +75,9 @@ static std::string GetVertexInterfaceDeclaration(bool is_output, bool use_clip_p
         out += "out gl_PerVertex {\n";
         // Apple Silicon GPU drivers optimize more aggressively, which can create
         // too much variance and cause visual artifacting in games like Pokemon.
-#ifdef __APPLE__
+        // PS5: the console's shader compiler does too (moving black stipples on Pokemon X's and
+        // Alpha Sapphire's outlined models, gone with this on a console: PS5CEMU-HAR #23).
+#if defined(__APPLE__) || defined(__PROSPERO__)
         out += "    invariant vec4 gl_Position;\n";
 #else
         out += "    vec4 gl_Position;\n";
